WEBVTT

NOTE Shift Work and Neighbourhood Life study. Interview T02 (participant P12, "Daniel", warehouse operative on fixed nights). Recorded over video call on 21 March 2024; exported from the meeting software as captions. Names of people and places are changed.

1
00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:07.480
Interviewer: Thanks for joining. Could you tell me a bit about your job and your hours?

2
00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:14.950
Daniel: Yeah, sure. I'm an operative at the distribution centre out by the ring road, picking and loading for the supermarket deliveries.

3
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:22.640
Daniel: I'm on fixed nights, ten at night to six in the morning, and I do six nights a fortnight, so three one week and three the next, with the days moving around a bit.

4
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:31.300
Daniel: I've been on nights for about five years. I asked for it, actually, because nights pay a premium of two pounds an hour and we needed the money when my daughter was born.

5
00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:36.700
Interviewer: And how does that fit with life on your street?

6
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:45.900
Daniel: Badly, if I'm honest. The main thing for me was the bus. Until March last year there was a twenty-two forty bus from the end of my road that got me to the depot for ten.

7
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:55.800
Daniel: The council cut that route in March 2023, and since then I have to drive, which costs more, or get a lift, and that's changed when I can do anything else in the evening.

8
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:07.200
Daniel: I used to coach the under-tens at the football club on Tuesday evenings, from six till half seven, and then get the bus to work. Once the bus went, I couldn't make it work with the driving and the parking, so I stopped coaching in the September.

9
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:19.900
Daniel: That's the thing I miss most, to be honest. Nobody at the club made me feel unwelcome, the opposite, they kept the job open for me for a whole season. It was purely the transport.

10
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:26.100
Interviewer: You said nobody at the club made you feel unwelcome. What about your neighbours more generally?

11
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:37.400
Daniel: That's different. With the neighbours, yeah, I did feel shut out, especially with the residents' group chat. Everything gets decided in the evening on the chat, between seven and ten, when I'm getting ready or driving in.

12
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:49.200
Daniel: By the time I wake up at two in the afternoon there are a hundred messages and the decision's made. When they voted on the speed bumps I didn't even know there was a vote until it was over.

13
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:58.200
Daniel: It's not just the chat either. The summer street party is planned on a Wednesday evening every year, and the litter pick is on a weekday at six, so I've never been to either.

13a
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:09.900
Daniel: My wife goes to the street party with our daughter, and people are friendly to her, but they don't really know who I am. A couple of neighbours thought she was a single mum until last year, which we laughed about, but it tells you something.

13b
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:21.600
Daniel: And there's a feeling, I don't know how to put it, that people who aren't around in the evening aren't really part of the street. The ones who are always at the meetings are the ones who get things done, and fair play to them, but it means the street gets run for people who work nine to five.

13c
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:33.300
Daniel: When the residents' group asked the council for a residents' parking scheme, the hours they asked for were eight till six, which is exactly when people like me need to park in the day after a night shift and sleep. Nobody thought about us because nobody on nights was there.

13d
00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Daniel: I raised it once, I said could they leave decisions open for a day so people on nights can say something, and one of the admins said that was too complicated. After that I just muted it. So yes, excluded is the right word there, I'd say.

13e
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:58.900
Daniel: The funny thing is, at work I'm in a team where everyone's on nights, so I've got a whole social life there, we have a darts team and a group chat of our own that's busy at four in the morning. It's just that it's not where I live.

14
00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:05.700
Interviewer: Some people we've spoken to say they're simply too tired after nights. Is that true for you?

15
00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:15.400
Daniel: Not really, no. I've been on nights long enough that my body's used to it. I sleep from seven till two pretty much every day, and I feel fine.

16
00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:24.900
Daniel: For me it isn't tiredness, it's timing and getting there. If the meetings were at four in the afternoon I'd be at every one.

17
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:30.100
Interviewer: Are you a member of a union at work?

18
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:39.300
Daniel: I am, yes, I joined in 2021. The union got us the night premium raised from one fifty to two pounds an hour in the last pay deal.

19
00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:48.000
Daniel: I go to the branch meetings when they're on a Saturday morning, which is maybe three times a year. That's the only meeting of anything I go to now.

20
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.200
Interviewer: Thank you, Daniel. That's really helpful.
